  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Solar-driven aquaculture technology in Vietnam</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Vietnam is a major producer and exporter of pangasius and shrimp worldwide, with more than 500,000 aquaculture farms located primarily in remote regions of the Mekong Delta. These farms rely on fossil fuel diesel generators to operate, which is not sustainable and hinders Vietnam&apos;s ability to meet its Paris Climate Agreement target by 2030.Therefore, this study will be conducted on the feasibility of using solar-powered aquaculture technology on small- and medium-sized farms in Vietnam. This shift towards renewable energy solutions can substantially decrease CO2 emissions and comply with the United Nations&apos; Sustainable Development Goal 13 of addressing climate change.However, traditional smallholder farmers may face an obstacle in the form of the initial investment required to implement solar solutions. As a result, the study also aims to provide a comprehensive understanding of farmers&apos; requirements for solar-driven aquaculture technology and the availability of solar-powered technology solutions in the private sector.The study will involve the participation of aquaculture technology-focused private sector firms from the Netherlands and Vietnam, as well as Can Tho University to leverage the expertise of Vietnam&apos;s young minds.</narrative>
  </description>
